 1 big thing: Global gas crunch 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: Axios research. Chart: Noah Bressner/Axios The Iran war is driving up fuel prices worldwide, leading to angry protests around the globe and straining the economies of countries that have nothing to do with the war.

 Why it matters: The price surge squeezes consumers and strains governments trying to contain the shock — especially poorer countries with little money to spare, Axios' Ben Geman reports. What to watch: With no resolution to the conflict in sight, many governments face a hard choice — spend more money to keep prices down or face the political consequences of higher fuel costs.

 Dozens of nations have taken steps to cap, subsidize or lower taxes on various fuels, the International Energy Agency says. Governments are also taking steps to reduce demand, including more remote work and limiting government travel. Go deeper . 

 
 
