 4. 🐊 Florida&#x27;s next hot spot 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Fort Lauderdale Beach Park in Fort Lauderdale, Fla., in October 2025. Photo: Daniel Kozin/AP Fort Lauderdale — which sits between Miami and Palm Beach — has long marketed itself as a viable alternative to those two cities. Now, the South Florida city is getting its moment, with a rush of new luxury condominium projects and corporate tenants.

 The intrigue: "Much of the work is planned and financed by deep-pocketed New York developers betting that Fort Lauderdale's time has finally come. ... New York-based firms are behind 4.6 million square feet of downtown development, roughly 45% of the total," Bloomberg reports. ✈️ Zoom in: Fort Lauderdale is getting transplants from big cities around the country, including New York City, Los Angeles and San Diego.

 The city's under-45 population is up 25% since 2020. "For some people, Miami became too hectic, too busy, too noisy," Miki Naftali, a New York developer with projects in Fort Lauderdale, told Bloomberg. "So Fort Lauderdale is a fantastic city for those that just want one notch down."

