 6. 💡 Nuclear reality check 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Shoshana Gordon/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Nuclear power's comeback is running into roadblocks, Axios' Alan Neuhauser writes. 

 Why it matters: Investors piled into nuclear energy companies, expecting returns to rise alongside the power-hungry AI data centers. But enthusiasm is turning into frustration with the industry's long timelines, high costs and intense engineering challenges. 👀 Case in point: This week, engineering firm Holtec postponed its $900 million IPO.

 Holtec wants to be the first U.S. company to restart a retired nuclear reactor — at Palisades Nuclear Plant in Michigan — and the pitch had enough appeal to garner up to $1.52 billion in federal loan guarantees. But restarting isn't simple: Holtec twice delayed the reactor's start date after finding the equipment was in worse-than-expected condition. Then federal inspectors found that Holtec wasn't following radiation-exposure regulations. Zoom out: Nuclear and uranium stocks on the S&amp;P 500 have plummeted nearly 29% since Jan. 1.

 Three advanced nuclear companies that went public this year have struggled: Fuel maker Standard Nuclear and reactor developers X-energy and Deep Fission are trading far below their list prices. 📉 The bottom line: Nuclear power could help ease the data-center power crunch, but it's not coming quickly.

